        "passage": "Cellular respiration is the set of metabolic reactions that convert biochemical energy from nutrients into ATP. In eukaryotes, this process is compartmentalized: glycolysis occurs in the cytosol, the citric acid (TCA) cycle takes place in the mitochondrial matrix, and oxidative phosphorylation occurs across the inner mitochondrial membrane. Glycolysis breaks down one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate, producing a net gain of two ATP and two NADH. The pathway consists of ten enzyme-catalyzed steps, three of which are irreversible and serve as regulatory points: hexokinase, phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1), and pyruvate kinase. PFK-1 catalyzes the phosphorylation of fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, an allosterically regulated commitment step. ATP and citrate inhibit PFK-1, whereas AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activate it. This regulation ensures that glycolysis proceeds only when the cell requires energy, preventing futile cycling with gluconeogenesis.\n\nUnder aerobic conditions, pyruvate is transported into the mitochondria and converted to acetyl-CoA by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, which links glycolysis to the TCA cycle. The TCA cycle oxidizes acetyl-CoA, generating three NADH, one FADH2, one GTP, and two CO2 per turn. These reduced coenzymes donate electrons to the electron transport chain, where a series of complexes (I-IV) establish a proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane. The flow of protons back through ATP synthase drives ATP synthesis. Typically, each NADH yields approximately 2.5 ATP, and each FADH2 yields approximately 1.5 ATP. Thus, the complete oxidation of one glucose molecule can yield about 30-32 ATP, with the majority produced by oxidative phosphorylation.\n\nIn anaerobic conditions, such as during intense exercise, the TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation are limited by oxygen availability. Cells rely on glycolysis for ATP, but the regeneration of NAD+ from NADH becomes critical. Pyruvate is reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase, regenerating NAD+ and allowing glycolysis to continue. This anaerobic pathway produces only two ATP per glucose but enables rapid ATP production when oxygen is scarce. The Cori cycle transports lactate to the liver, where it is converted back to glucose via gluconeogenesis, an energy-costly process that requires six ATP equivalents per glucose.\n\nEnergy metabolism is tightly regulated by hormonal signals and substrate availability. Insulin promotes glycolysis and the TCA cycle, whereas glucagon and epinephrine promote gluconeogenesis and glycogen breakdown. The regulation of key enzymes ensures metabolic homeostasis. For instance, pyruvate kinase, which catalyzes the final step of glycolysis, is activated by fructose-1,6-bisphosphate and inhibited by ATP and alanine. In contrast, gluconeogenesis must bypass the irreversible steps of glycolysis. Pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate, and phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase converts oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate. Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase and glucose-6-phosphatase catalyze the other bypass reactions. These reciprocal regulatory mechanisms prevent simultaneous glycolysis and gluconeogenesis, a state known as futile cycling."
