        "passage": "Chemical equilibrium is a dynamic state in which the rates of the forward and reverse reactions are equal, resulting in no net change in the concentrations of reactants and products over time. For a general reaction aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, the equilibrium constant K is defined as K = [C]^c[D]^d / ([A]^a[B]^b), where the concentrations are those at equilibrium. K is dimensionless and depends only on temperature, not on the initial concentrations or the presence of catalysts. A large K (>>1) indicates that at equilibrium, products are favored; a small K (<<1) indicates that reactants are favored.\n\nWhen a reaction mixture is not at equilibrium, the same expression, using current concentrations, is called the reaction quotient Q. Comparing Q to K reveals the direction in which the reaction must proceed to reach equilibrium. If Q < K, the reaction will proceed in the forward direction (toward products) to increase Q until it equals K. If Q > K, the reaction will proceed in the reverse direction (toward reactants) to decrease Q. If Q = K, the system is at equilibrium. A useful mnemonic is to draw the relationship alphabetically: K < Q means the reaction shifts left; K > Q means it shifts right.\n\nLe Chatelier's principle states that if an external stress is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system will shift to partially relieve that stress and reestablish equilibrium. External stresses include changes in concentration, pressure, volume, and temperature. For example, adding more reactant will shift the equilibrium to the right, consuming some of the added reactant and producing more product. Removing a product will also shift the equilibrium to the right. Conversely, adding product or removing reactant shifts the equilibrium to the left. For gaseous reactions, changing pressure or volume affects equilibrium only if the number of moles of gas changes between reactants and products. Increasing pressure (by decreasing volume) shifts the equilibrium toward the side with fewer moles of gas. Adding an inert gas at constant volume does not change partial pressures or concentrations and thus has no effect on equilibrium. Temperature changes alter the value of K itself; for an exothermic reaction, increasing temperature decreases K, while for an endothermic reaction, increasing temperature increases K. Catalysts do not affect the position of equilibrium; they only increase the rate at which equilibrium is reached. Pure solids and pure liquids do not appear in the equilibrium expression because their concentrations are effectively constant. Therefore, adding or removing a pure solid or liquid does not shift equilibrium.\n\nUnderstanding these principles is essential for predicting the behavior of chemical systems and for optimizing industrial processes, such as the Haber process for ammonia synthesis, where conditions are chosen to maximize yield while considering rate and cost."
