        "topic": "Electrostatics and Magnetism",
        "subject": "Physics",
        "passage": "Electrostatics and magnetism are two fundamental branches of classical electromagnetism that describe forces between charged particles and the behavior of magnetic fields. Although they are unified in modern physics through Maxwell's equations, the MCAT treats them as distinct topics with specific principles and applications.\n\nCoulomb's law quantifies the electrostatic force between two point charges. The force is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. The electric field is defined as the force per unit charge experienced by a small positive test charge. Electric potential is the potential energy per unit charge at a point in space. Equipotential lines represent regions of constant electric potential; no work is done moving a charge along an equipotential line.\n\nMagnetism is characterized by magnetic fields, which exert forces on moving charges and magnetic dipoles. The magnetic force on a moving charge is perpendicular to both the velocity of the charge and the magnetic field, and its magnitude is proportional to the charge, the speed, the magnetic field strength, and the sine of the angle between velocity and field. Unlike electric forces, magnetic forces do no work on a moving charge because the force is always perpendicular to the displacement.\n\nA common experimental setup in electrostatics and magnetism involves a cathode ray tube, which demonstrates the motion of charged particles in electric and magnetic fields. In one such experiment, electrons are accelerated from rest through a potential difference and then travel through a region containing either a uniform electric field or a uniform magnetic field. The deflection of the electron beam can be monitored to determine properties of the fields or the particles themselves.\n\nIn this experiment, an electron gun accelerates electrons through a potential difference of 250 V. The electrons emerge with kinetic energy equal to the work done by the electric field, which is the product of the electron charge and the potential difference. After acceleration, the electrons enter a region of uniform magnetic field directed into the page. The magnetic field causes the electrons to follow a circular path with a radius that depends on their speed, mass, charge, and the magnetic field strength.\n\nTo investigate the effect of an electric field instead, the same electron beam passes between two parallel plates separated by 2 cm and connected to a battery that creates a uniform electric field between them. The electric field exerts a constant force on the electrons, causing them to accelerate perpendicular to their initial direction, resulting in a parabolic deflection.\n\nThe experiment can be used to measure the charge-to-mass ratio of the electron, a fundamental constant. By balancing the electric and magnetic fields so that the electron beam passes undeflected, the speed of the electrons can be determined from the ratio of the electric field to the magnetic field. Combining this speed with the measured radius of the circular path in the magnetic field alone yields the charge-to-mass ratio.\n\nThe principles of electrostatics and magnetism are also applied in more complex systems, such as electromagnetic flow meters, which measure the velocity of a conductive fluid by applying a magnetic field and detecting the induced electric field perpendicular to both the flow and the magnetic field. This application, though not part of the described experiment, demonstrates the practical relevance of these fundamental laws."
