        "passage": "Human memory is not a single, monolithic system but rather a collection of interconnected processes that operate in concert to encode, store, and retrieve information. The standard model of memory posits three main stages: encoding, storage, and retrieval. Encoding is the initial processing of incoming information, transforming sensory input into a neural representation. Storage refers to the maintenance of that representation over time, and retrieval is the process of accessing stored information when needed.\n\nMemory is often categorized into distinct types based on duration and consciousness. Sensory memory holds raw sensory information for a fraction of a second, allowing for the perception of continuity. Iconic memory is the visual sensory memory, while echoic memory is the auditory counterpart. Information that is attended to can be transferred to short-term memory, which has limited capacity and holds information for about 20 to 30 seconds unless it is rehearsed. Working memory is an extension of short-term memory that involves active manipulation of information, such as mental arithmetic. Through processes like rehearsal and deep semantic processing, information can be consolidated into long-term memory, which has essentially unlimited capacity and can persist for years.\n\nLong-term memory is further divided into explicit (declarative) and implicit (nondeclarative) memory. Explicit memory involves conscious recollection and includes episodic memory (personal events) and semantic memory (facts and general knowledge). Implicit memory involves unconscious influences on behavior, such as procedural skills (e.g., riding a bike) and priming. The hippocampus, located in the medial temporal lobe, is critical for the consolidation of explicit memories, whereas the frontal lobes are involved in working memory and the strategic organization of memories.\n\nMemory is not always reliable and can be influenced by interference. Proactive interference occurs when previously learned information impedes the learning and retrieval of new information. For example, if you learn a new password after years of using an old one, the old password may interfere with your ability to recall the new one. This type of interference is more likely when the old and new information are similar. Spaced repetition, a study technique that involves reviewing information at increasing intervals, is designed to enhance long-term encoding by strengthening memory traces.\n\nResearch on memory has also examined how memory systems interact. A study by Glanzer and Cunitz (1966) demonstrated the serial position effect, where participants recall the first and last items of a list better than middle items. The primacy effect is attributed to better encoding of initial items into long-term memory, while the recency effect is thought to reflect items still present in short-term memory. This pattern suggests that short-term and long-term memory are distinct systems, as disrupting short-term memory (e.g., by imposing a delay) abolishes the recency effect but leaves the primacy effect intact."
