        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "The passage consistently argues that the pandemic and lockdowns widened the economic gap, harming small businesses while benefiting large corporations. Choice B accurately reflects this central thesis. Choice A is contradicted by the passage's suggestion that the consequences may have been intentional. Choice C is a point made by critics but not the author's main argument. Choice D is challenged by the passage's description of limited aid.",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "section": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills",
        "topic": "Economics and Business",
        "subject": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills (CARS)",
        "passage": "The pandemic and the associated lockdowns have been described by many as a great equalizer, but a closer look at the economic fallout reveals a starkly different reality. As governments around the world ordered the closure of all but 'essential' businesses, a clear pattern emerged: large corporate entities, particularly those with significant online retail and technology operations, were deemed essential and allowed to continue operating, while countless small and medium-sized enterprises were forced to shut their doors. This disparity was not merely a matter of public health policy; it had profound economic consequences that reshaped the competitive landscape.\n\nConsider the case of a family-owned bus repair business that had been operating for four decades. When lockdowns brought public transit to a near standstill, the demand for bus repairs plummeted. The owner, like many small business owners, had invested his life savings into the enterprise and had no alternative source of income. He was denied the right to earn a living, while the wealth of the founders of major tech and retail corporations reached record levels. These giants, often labeled 'essential,' saw their revenues surge as consumers flocked to online platforms for goods and services, from groceries to electronics. The result was a dramatic concentration of wealth in the hands of a few, while the competition was systematically weakened or eliminated.\n\nThe pattern was not accidental. Some observers argue that lockdowns were designed not primarily to protect public health, but to accelerate a pre-existing trend toward economic consolidation. By destroying independent livelihoods, the state creates a population that is increasingly dependent on large corporations and, ultimately, on government support. This dependency, in turn, undermines the autonomy that is essential for a healthy democracy. The closure of 'mom and pop' shops was not a side effect but a goal, as it eliminated the economic independence that could threaten the power of both the state and corporate elites.\n\nHowever, this narrative is not without its critics. Some economists contend that the shift toward large businesses was a natural market response to changing consumer behavior, which had been accelerating for years before the pandemic. They argue that small businesses were already struggling to compete with the convenience and pricing of online retailers, and the pandemic simply hastened an inevitable process. Moreover, they point out that many large corporations also suffered during the crisis, and that government aid programs, such as the Paycheck Protection Program in the United States, were designed to help small businesses survive.\n\nWhile it is true that some small businesses did receive government assistance, the scope and accessibility of such programs were often limited. Many small business owners found the application processes cumbersome and the funds insufficient to cover their fixed costs. In contrast, large corporations with sophisticated legal and financial teams were better positioned to navigate the complex regulatory environment and to capitalize on the opportunities created by the crisis. The pandemic, far from being a great equalizer, has widened the gap between the economic elite and the rest of society, raising troubling questions about the future of economic opportunity and the concentration of power."
