        "explanation": "The researcher's argument emphasizes that threat displays are learned symbols that carry meaning and reinforce social order. This aligns with the passage's description of microsociology, which focuses on the meanings attached to gestures and symbols. Option A is unrelated to baboon behavior. Option C is about conflict theory, which focuses on resource competition, not symbolic meaning. Option D is about functionalism, which focuses on system stability, but the researcher's emphasis is on the symbolic and learned nature of the display, not its function in maintaining stability.",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "section": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills",
        "topic": "Sociology and Anthropology",
        "subject": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills (CARS)",
        "passage": "The study of human societies often begins with the recognition that every social arrangement has both intended and unintended consequences. Consider, for example, a national program of old-age insurance commonly known as Social Security. Its manifest function is to provide a financial safety net for retirees and lower-income workers, ensuring that they do not fall into destitution after their earning years end. Yet the same program may also have latent functions that are less obvious: it can, for instance, create a large administrative apparatus that provides stable employment for thousands of claims processors, actuaries, and customer-service representatives. At the same time, latent dysfunctions may emerge. The centralized storage of personal data, while intended to streamline benefit disbursement, has created new opportunities for identity theft, a harm that was never part of the program's stated goals. Thus, a single institution can simultaneously serve its declared purpose, sustain an unintended occupational niche, and generate novel forms of social harm.\n\nSociologists have long debated which level of analysis best captures such dynamics. Microsociology focuses on face-to-face interactions and the meanings people attach to gestures, symbols, and words. A hand signal that is friendly in one cultural context—say, the American peace sign formed with the index and middle fingers—may be a grave insult in another. Understanding why such a gesture carries different meanings requires close attention to the local symbolic codes that structure everyday life. Macrosociology, by contrast, examines large-scale structures such as class, race, and political institutions, asking how these systems allocate resources and power across a whole society.\n\nThe distinction between micro and macro perspectives is not merely academic; it shapes the kinds of questions researchers ask. A biologist who studies two groups of baboons on an island might observe that one group has successfully claimed the only freshwater spring and the richest grove of fruit trees. When the less fortunate group approaches, the healthier baboons growl menacingly. A researcher working within a conflict-theory framework would see this as evidence of competition over scarce resources, a struggle that mirrors human patterns of inequality. A functionalist, in contrast, might emphasize how the threat display maintains order and reduces actual physical violence, thereby preserving the stability of the whole baboon population. A symbolic interactionist would be more interested in how the growl and other gestures are learned and interpreted within the baboon community.\n\nThese theoretical lenses are not mutually exclusive, but they do lead researchers to notice different aspects of social life. Conflict theorists remind us that resources are rarely distributed equally and that those who control them often use force or the threat of force to maintain their advantage. Functionalists highlight the ways in which social arrangements—even unequal ones—can contribute to the smooth operation of a system. Symbolic interactionists insist that no social arrangement can be understood without decoding the shared meanings that make it possible. The choice of lens, therefore, is itself a statement about what the researcher believes is most important to know about the social world."
